
### 101 proposition prop:final-package-shape-convention [Final package-shape convention]
\begin{proposition}[Final package-shape convention] \label{prop:final-package-shape-convention} For the current presentation package there is a single source-level package shape. The primary article is \path{submission_abstract.tex}; the selected technical supplement is \path{main.tex}; the selected bounded theorem-obligation ledger is \path{theorem_inventory.json}; and the selected supplemental support surface is exact inclusion of \path{review_bundle/} with the final digest record \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}. No archive locator, DOI-bearing replacement, or alternate supplemental directory is part of the current package convention. Consequently every bundle-backed support claim in this source is accepted only under exact inclusion of that directory with matching final digests; without that external inclusion record it remains a local package-support claim. \end{proposition}

### 162 definition def:architecture-axis-map [Architecture-axis map]
\begin{definition}[Architecture-axis map] \label{def:architecture-axis-map} The compiler architecture uses five disjoint evidence axes. An accepted claim may read evidence from an axis only at the admissible evidence level shown in the displayed axis map; the negative boundary in the last column is part of the claim certificate and cannot be discharged by prose from another axis. \begin{center} \small \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{p{.16\textwidth}p{.27\textwidth}p{.27\textwidth}X} \toprule Axis & Role in this paper & Admissible evidence & Negative boundary \\ \midrule BEDC source theory & finite-kernel theory, proof obligations, and implementation contracts & pinned source paths, commits, imported labels, bounded summaries, and source-interface records & not a publication acceptance record, not a fresh rebuild, and not a complete BEDC exposition \\ Lean markers & formal-verification records for named obligations & recorded build, axiom-purity, marker-existence, or source-interface rows when named by the package & not object-language closure, not discovery by itself, and not proof of unrelated claims \\ Finite witnesses & manifest/evaluator substrate evidence for artifacts and package certificates & manifest rows, evaluator outcomes, bundled snapshots, verifier logs, file digests, and limitation ledgers & not semantic universality, not whole-tree dynamic validation, and not an unbounded artifact rerun \\ Agents & proposal, search, review, ranking, and repair assistance & advisory drafts, review reports, scheduler scores, and gate-input suggestions after being recorded as such & not proof evidence, not human promotion, not novelty certification, and not gate passage \\ Automath publication compiler & intake, route, overlap, submission-pack, and support surface governance &...

### 201 proposition prop:architecture-axis-non-escalation [Axis non-escalation]
\begin{proposition}[Axis non-escalation] \label{prop:architecture-axis-non-escalation} In any record-gate-sound package using Definition~\ref{def:architecture-axis-map}, passage or evidence on one architecture axis cannot certify a load-bearing claim whose claim kind requires another axis. More precisely, if a claim certificate uses an axis $A$ but its stronger reading needs an evidence item or negative-boundary discharge listed only on a different axis $A'$, then the stronger reading is not accepted unless the package contains a separate submitted coordinate for $A'$ and the gates assigned to that coordinate pass. Consequently Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} constructs only finite publication certificates whose axis coordinates are explicitly present in the submitted support surface; it does not infer BEDC closure from Lean markers, artifact semantics from manifest rows, proof evidence from agents, venue upload from automath route state, or support-surface availability from local bundle paths. \end{proposition}

### 361 lemma lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness [Source-interface extraction correctness]
\begin{lemma}[Source-interface extraction correctness] \label{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} For the pinned newmath repository path \path{papers/bedc/parts/visions/audit_map_methodology/automated_theory_discovery_pipeline_calculus.tex} at commit \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b}, Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record} is the complete imported interface used by this paper at the manual path-verified level and only at that level. Its extraction certificate is: manual line-range audit against the pinned file, mirrored in \path{source_interface_record.json}, extraction date 2026-06-01, and summary mode ``bounded paraphrase'' rather than replacement of the full source statements. Consequently every use below of an imported discovery-gate label is limited to the corresponding row's path, commit, label, line range, and bounded summary. Even when an explicitly named executable finite record comparison is also invoked, the upgraded content is only equality and containment of those finite fields; exact source statements are not imported. In particular, the lemma is not a fresh Lean/BEDC rebuild, not an axiom-purity certificate, and not a semantic proof of the imported source theorem statements. \end{lemma}

### 400 proposition prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface [Reviewable source-snapshot interface]
\begin{proposition}[Reviewable source-snapshot interface] \label{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface} The imported discovery-gate spine used in the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, has a reviewer-facing source interface. Namely, the tuple consisting of the pinned local source path, commit \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b}, Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}, \path{source_interface_record.json}, and the supplemental copy \path{review_bundle/source_snapshots/automated_theory_discovery_pipeline_calculus_3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b.tex} is sufficient to inspect every imported source label used by this paper at the path-verified evidence level. The distribution mode for review is supplemental upload of \path{review_bundle/} with the presentation-only submission. No public archive URL or DOI is part of the current theorem interface. \end{proposition}

### 430 proposition prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency [Executable source-interface record comparison]
\begin{proposition}[Executable source-interface record comparison] \label{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} The source-interface table, its JSON mirror, and the bundled source snapshot give one record-level imported discovery-gate interface for the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}. The record fields are as follows: the source commit is \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b}; the bundled snapshot is \path{review_bundle/source_snapshots/automated_theory_discovery_pipeline_calculus_3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b.tex}; the finite comparison script is \path{review_bundle/verify_source_interface_record.py}; the recorded successful comparison log is \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log}; the byte count recorded for the source and snapshot is 20746; the recorded SHA-256 digest checked by the script is \path{a492900222dd66d154cdd5a79d2b2ed0b856ad1ec59e5dfce55dbc21875ed1b2}; the recorded snapshot length is 498 lines; for each of the sixteen line ranges in \path{source_interface_record.json}, the corresponding imported label is recorded inside the stated snapshot range; and the sixteen triples \emph{label, line range, bounded summary} in Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record} are the sixteen JSON records. Hence, at the finite record-comparison level, the manuscript table, JSON record, review-bundle manifest, digest, line count, and bundled snapshot define one consistent imported-source interface. The comparison does not assert semantic truth of the imported source theorems and does not replace the bounded paraphrase discipline of Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}. \end{proposition}

### 485 proposition prop:source-interface-evidence-level-separation [Source-interface evidence-level separation]
\begin{proposition}[Source-interface evidence-level separation] \label{prop:source-interface-evidence-level-separation} Let $r$ be any accepted manuscript claim whose discovery component cites one of the imported labels in Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}. If $r$ uses only Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}, then the imported-label premise of $r$ is accepted only at the manual path-verified bounded-paraphrase level. If $r$ additionally cites Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}, then the same premise is upgraded only by the finite record comparisons listed in that proposition: table--JSON equality, manifest agreement, snapshot digest and line count agreement, and label containment in the declared ranges. In neither case does the premise become a fresh Lean/BEDC rebuild, a proof of the semantic truth of the imported source theorem, or an unbounded restatement of the source text. Consequently the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, can use the imported discovery-gate spine only through these two explicitly separated evidence levels. \end{proposition}

### 547 definition def:operational-acceptance-interface [Operational acceptance interface]
\begin{definition}[Operational acceptance interface] \label{def:operational-acceptance-interface} A \emph{claim} is a manuscript assertion that can affect a mathematical, artifact, route, or submission decision. A \emph{load-bearing claim} is a claim whose removal would change a theorem statement, a case-study conclusion, or the submission-readiness status. An \emph{advisory output} is any AI-agent draft, score, suggested theorem, review packet, or repair proposal before a gate records it. A \emph{deterministic gate} is a repeatable predicate, script, or checklist whose inputs and pass/fail decision are recorded independently of the prose generated by an agent. A \emph{human promotion} is the explicit editorial act that moves a candidate into publication state, for example by creating an active \texttt{2026\_*} paper directory with \texttt{main.tex} and \texttt{PIPELINE.md}. An \emph{evidence surface} is the set of source paths, commits, commands, logs, inventories, and witness records cited for a claim. An \emph{acceptance entry} is a manuscript or submission-ledger record that marks a candidate claim as usable in the publication-facing argument. \emph{Accepted work} is the subset of candidate claims with such an entry. This is a status notion only: the definition records that an editorial acceptance entry exists, but it does not by itself assert external editorial acceptance or implementation correctness. A pipeline is \emph{record-gate-sound for acceptance} when each acceptance entry records the claim kind, the evidence surface, the evidence level, and a pass record for every gate in $\Gamma$ assigned to that claim kind, and when a route-boundary entry also records human promotion. The deterministic dispatch function $\Gamma$ from claim kinds to gates is part of the ...

### 696 definition def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table [Certificate-schema acceptance table]
\begin{definition}[Certificate-schema acceptance table] \label{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} Fix the seven-kind dispatch table $\Gamma$ of Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}. The verifier input for a load-bearing claim $c$ is the following finite record type: \[ \mathcal C(c)= \{K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B\}. \] Its typed fields are these and only these: $K$ is a finite set of claim kinds; $\ell\in\{\mathsf{path},\mathsf{command}\}$ is the declared evidence level; $E$ is a finite evidence-surface list; $H$ is the route-boundary field; $R$ is a finite family of gate pass records, each with gate name, input surface, decision, evidence pointer, and evidence level; $D$ is either empty or a finite list of pinned discovery source-interface rows; and $B$ is the command-boundary field. This is a data schema, not a theorem: all later exactness claims must be proved from this displayed record and cannot appeal to unlisted package state. Define $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ to be the conjunction of the six finite table predicates A1--A6 displayed below; no clause may query generated prose, a live daemon, an artifact evaluator, a proof assistant, or a venue state outside these fields and their cited submitted surfaces. \begin{center} \centering \small \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{p{0.08\linewidth}p{0.22\linewidth}X} \toprule Clause & Field read & Acceptance condition \\ \midrule A1 & $K,\ell$ & $K$ is a nonempty subset of the seven-kind domain and $\ell$ is one of the two evidence levels. \\ A2 & $E$ & Every evidence item is a submitted path, review-bundle path, manifest row, log path, pinned commit, or explicit historical-evidence boundary available at level $\ell$. \\ A3 & $K,R,E$ & For every gate in $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, $R$ has a pass record wi...

### 736 proposition prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier [Finite certificate schema verifier as a record check]
\begin{proposition}[Finite certificate schema verifier as a record check] \label{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} For every load-bearing manuscript claim $c$ whose component kinds lie in the seven-kind domain, $V(c,\mathcal C)$ is a finite, prose-insensitive record-schema verifier for the declared publication certificate. If $V$ accepts, then $\mathcal C(c)$ is locally checkable at exactly its declared evidence level. This is certificate-record verification only: it checks finite record shape, dispatch, evidence pointers, and command-boundary discipline, not end-to-end external semantic validation of the referenced artifacts or source theory. If $V$ rejects, then a reviewer can extract a finite bad subrecord consisting of the first failed schema check and the fields on which that check depends. \end{proposition}

### 780 theorem thm:record-acceptance-exactness [Finite-record acceptance exactness]
\begin{theorem}[Finite-record acceptance exactness] \label{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} Fix the seven-kind dispatch table $\Gamma$, the two evidence levels, and the typed certificate tuple $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ of Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}. For every load-bearing claim $c$ and every finite candidate certificate $\mathcal C(c)$, the finite verifier $V$ of Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} accepts $\mathcal C(c)$ if and only if the six-clause table predicate $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ holds. Moreover, if $V$ accepts a record for $c$, the same record cannot certify any stronger reading $c^{+}$ that adds a new claim kind, a stronger evidence level, an omitted route crossing, an omitted discovery interface, an omitted pass record, or a command-run assertion: such a reading has a finite bad subrecord of the corresponding clause. Thus $V$ is exact for this paper's finite record-acceptance relation over the submitted source, tables, manifests, logs, and explicitly declared historical boundaries, while remaining silent about external semantic validity of the referenced theory or artifacts and about the implementation-level soundness of any deployed gate implementation beyond the recorded pass rows. \end{theorem}

### 835 theorem thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary [Finite-coordinate determination boundary]
\begin{theorem}[Finite-coordinate determination boundary] \label{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and the verifier $V$. Let $F$ be the finite set of coordinates read by $V$ from a certificate record, namely the claim-kind set, declared evidence level, evidence surface, promotion field, gate-pass rows, discovery-source rows, and command-boundary tuple. If two ambient pipeline or artifact states induce the same values of all coordinates in $F$ for a claim $c$, then they induce the same record-acceptance decision and the same finite publication certificate for $c$. Consequently no property depending on a coordinate outside $F$---for example a fresh Lean build result, a daemon trace, an artifact semantic fact, or an external venue decision---is a consequence of the accepted certificate unless that property is first encoded as an additional finite certificate coordinate and the enlarged record is accepted. \end{theorem}

### 874 proposition prop:machine-readable-certificate-records [Machine-readable certificate records]
\begin{proposition}[Machine-readable certificate records] \label{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} For the current presentation package, the finite record checker $V$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}, with the record-acceptance semantics of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, has an executable record-level instance over the review bundle, conditional on exact supplemental inclusion of \path{review_bundle/} in the completed submission with matching final digests, as stated in Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}. The schema file \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json}, the pass-record ledger \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json}, the primary-interface map \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json}, the primary-claim inventory \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, the verifier script \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}, and the recorded run \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} instantiate the verifier at the path-verified level for entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$. Each pass record in the ledger has the five fields \emph{gate name}, \emph{input surface}, \emph{decision}, \emph{evidence pointer}, and \emph{evidence level}. The machine-readable submission-interface map is locked to rows \path{sim1}--\path{sim4} of Table~\ref{tab:submission-interface-map} by its row identifiers, certificates, surfaces, and evidence levels; the primary-claim inventory is locked to rows \path{pc1}--\path{pc12} by its row identifiers, source locations, row classes, and evidence levels. These executable locks check the finite record interface; full semantic equality between prose paraphrases and mathematical meaning, soundness of the gate implementations, and...

### 964 theorem thm:executable-record-non-escalation [Executable record non-escalation]
\begin{theorem}[Executable record non-escalation] \label{thm:executable-record-non-escalation} Let $F$ be the finite field projection read by \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}: manifest-field presence, entries $a_1$--$a_6$, claim-kind membership, evidence-level membership, assigned-gate coverage, pass-record fields, evidence-level agreement, discovery-field discipline, path-verified boundary status, row locks for \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}, and row-class locks for \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}. Assuming the exact-or-archive-equivalent review-bundle support surface of Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, among all assertions about the current review bundle that are functions only of this projection, Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} proves the maximal executable claim: the six current-package certificates satisfy the finite record-acceptance relation of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} at the declared path-verified fields, and no accepted row carries a command-run boundary. In particular, the executable verifier cannot certify any strictly stronger assertion whose truth depends on prose-to-semantics equality, independent extraction of claims from the manuscript, completeness of paths not declared in the records, or the external semantics of a Lean development, publication daemon, artifact suite, or evaluator. \end{theorem}

### 1017 theorem thm:executable-certificate-construction-invariant [Executable certificate-construction invariant]
\begin{theorem}[Executable certificate-construction invariant] \label{thm:executable-certificate-construction-invariant} For the submitted review bundle, let $G$ be the union of the finite fields read by the two executable verifiers \path{review_bundle/verify_source_interface_record.py} and \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}. Thus $G$ contains the ten source-interface table triples, the JSON source-interface records, the pinned source snapshot path, digest, line count, declared source commit and line ranges, the six current-package pass-record entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$, the submission-map rows \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}, the primary-inventory row locks \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}, and the two recorded successful verifier logs. On the equivalence class of packages with the same projection $G$, the publication-safety interface has a unique executable finite certificate construction: the imported discovery-gate interface is the sixteen-row checked source interface of Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}, the accepted current-package certificates are exactly the six records of Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, and any stronger reading that changes the source interface, adds an accepted row, raises the evidence level, adds a command-run assertion, or asserts external semantic or venue correctness is not determined by $G$ and is rejected at the finite-record boundary unless new certificate fields and verifier records are supplied. \end{theorem}

### 1079 proposition prop:claim-kind-dispatch [Deterministic claim-kind dispatch]
\begin{proposition}[Deterministic claim-kind dispatch] \label{prop:claim-kind-dispatch} For every load-bearing manuscript claim whose kind lies in the seven-kind domain above, $\Gamma$ assigns a unique finite set of deterministic gates. In a pipeline that is record-gate-sound for acceptance, a mixed load-bearing claim may be used as accepted work only if its acceptance entry records passage through every gate in the union assigned to its component kinds. \end{proposition}

### 1100 theorem thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary [Assigned-gate soundness audit boundary]
\begin{theorem}[Assigned-gate soundness audit boundary] \label{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary} Let $P$ be a finite submitted package with pass-record ledger $L$ and dispatch table $\Gamma$. For a load-bearing claim $c$, write $G(c)=\bigcup_{k\in K(c)}\Gamma(k)$ for the finite set of gates assigned to its claim-kind set. The package may use a ledger row for $c$ as a sound record-gate witness exactly at the following finite-record strength: \begin{enumerate} \item for every $g\in G(c)$ the submitted ledger contains a pass row for $g$, including the gate name, input surface, decision, evidence pointer, and evidence level; \item every evidence pointer named by those pass rows denotes a submitted path, manifest row, theorem/proposition/table label, uploaded bundle file, or explicitly declared historical boundary at the evidence level stated in the row; and \item if the manuscript asserts any stronger proposition that the underlying gate implementation was independently sound, freshly executed, or semantically correct beyond the recorded finite schema check, the submitted package also contains a gate-soundness audit tuple for that stronger proposition: source path, command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path for the assigned gate. \end{enumerate} Consequently, in the absence of the audit tuple in (3), record-gate soundness is an explicit hypothesis of the publication-safety theorem, not a theorem proved by the current package. The finite pass rows prove only the package-level statement that no accepted ledger claim omits an assigned gate row or its declared evidence surface. \end{theorem}

### 1163 theorem thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton [Finite certificate obstruction skeleton]
\begin{theorem}[Finite certificate obstruction skeleton] \label{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} Let $c$ be a load-bearing manuscript claim whose component kinds lie in the seven-kind domain. The verifier of Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} gives the following complete audit classification at the declared evidence level. A certificate for $c$ passes the publication-safety interface if and only if it has no bad subrecord of the following five types: \begin{enumerate} \item missing evidence surface for the declared evidence level; \item missing pass record for a gate in $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$; \item missing human-promotion record when the claim crosses from intake or source material into active publication work; \item discovery escalation without the pinned discovery-gate source-interface rows for certificate, ledger, lineage, and boundary witnesses; or \item command-run escalation without command, source commit, environment, exit code, and log path. \end{enumerate} Equivalently, every unsafe accepted claim contains an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord of one of these five types, and deleting all other fields preserves the same rejection by the verifier. This is an audit-skeleton theorem for the software-workshop route: it classifies finite certificate failures and makes no claim about theorem-proving complexity or about fresh execution of the underlying workflow. \end{theorem}

### 1264 theorem thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form [Finite extension-budget normal form]
\begin{theorem}[Finite extension-budget normal form] \label{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$, the finite certificate schema, and an accepted finite certificate \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) for a load-bearing package claim $c$. Let $c^{+}$ be a proposed stronger reading of the same row which is not record-equivalent to $c$ in the sense of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}. Then $c^{+}$ is accepted by the publication-safety interface if and only if the submitted finite record surface contains an extension record in the following normal form: \[ \Delta(c,c^{+})=(\Delta K,\Delta E,\Delta H,\Delta R,\Delta D,\Delta B), \] where the nonempty coordinates are exactly the coordinates needed by the stronger reading: \begin{enumerate} \item $\Delta K$ names every additional claim-kind component asserted by $c^{+}$; \item $\Delta E$ supplies the submitted evidence surfaces for those added components at the declared evidence level; \item $\Delta R$ supplies pass records for every gate in \(\bigcup_{k\in K\cup\Delta K}\Gamma(k)\) not already supplied by $R$; \item $\Delta H$ supplies any newly asserted promotion, route, venue, or external instantiation boundary; \item $\Delta D$ supplies the pinned discovery-source rows whenever the stronger reading adds discovery, classifier-shift, or theorem-content-delta content; and \item $\Delta B$ supplies the command, source-state, environment, exit-code, and log-path tuple whenever the stronger reading raises a path-verified row to command-run strength. \end{enumerate} Equivalently, every genuine strengthening has positive finite extension budget: at least one coordinate of \(\Delta(c,c^{+})\) must be nonempty, and every nonempty obstruction class from Theorem~\ref{thm:f...

### 1351 proposition prop:gate-interface-separation [Gate-interface separation]
\begin{proposition}[Gate-interface separation] \label{prop:gate-interface-separation} The promotion, overlap, submission-state, and theorem-content delta gates are separate deterministic interfaces. None can be replaced by passage of another one when a load-bearing claim has the corresponding component. \end{proposition}

### 1375 proposition prop:scheduler-selection-advisory [Scheduler selection is advisory before discovery gating]
\begin{proposition}[Scheduler selection is advisory before discovery gating] \label{prop:scheduler-selection-advisory} Let $s$ be a scheduler or critical-path score attached to a candidate claim $c$. If $s$ recommends selecting $c$, then the recommendation is a work-selection claim and by itself can only pass the scheduler gate. If $c$ is also asserted to be a discovery or classifier-shifting contribution in a record-gate-sound pipeline, then the accepted mixed claim must pass both the scheduler gate and the discovery gate. Consequently, critical-path scores may prioritize review order, and the imported classifier-shift selection rule may break ties among otherwise equal discovery potential coordinates, but neither score nor recommendation accepts a classifier-shift discovery unless the discovery gate also supplies the required certificate, ledger, and lineage witnesses. \end{proposition}

### 1420 theorem thm:publication-safety-interface [Axis-and-level certificate invariant under record-gate soundness]
\begin{theorem}[Axis-and-level certificate invariant under record-gate soundness] \label{thm:publication-safety-interface} Consider a theory-to-paper pipeline with the operational interface above and a finite submitted record surface consisting only of the manuscript source, printed tables, submitted manifests, uploaded review-bundle records, recorded logs, and explicitly declared historical boundaries. Assume the following four distinct finite-record hypotheses. \begin{enumerate} \item \emph{Acceptance entries}: every load-bearing claim in an active manuscript is either rejected or has an acceptance entry with an evidence surface inside this finite submitted record surface. \item \emph{Record-gate soundness}: in the sense of Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}, a recorded acceptance entry is present only after every gate assigned by $\Gamma$ to the claim kind has already produced a pass record in that finite record surface, every advisory output is demoted unless the deterministic gate set $\Gamma$ for its claim kind passes, and no independent implementation-soundness, fresh-execution, or semantic-correctness claim about the gates is inferred unless the package also contains the corresponding source path, command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path. \item \emph{Discovery certificate input}: discovery claims are checked through the pinned discovery-gate interface certified by Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} and Propositions~\ref{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface}, \ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}, and \ref{prop:source-interface-evidence-level-separation}, in particular the separated manual/path-verified and executable finite-record levels for the pipeline safety and main pipeline the...

### 1570 proposition prop:publication-safety-maximality [Finite-record maximality]
\begin{proposition}[Finite-record maximality] \label{prop:publication-safety-maximality} Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, the constructed certificate is maximal with respect to the finite submitted record surface. More precisely, let $c$ be an accepted load-bearing claim and let $\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ be the certificate constructed in that theorem. If a stronger reading $c^{+}$ asserts any external end-to-end compiler correctness, current execution of unrecorded gates, proof-assistant correctness, current formal-source currency, dynamic artifact semantics, or venue acceptance, then either $c^{+}$ is not a distinct finite-record consequence of $\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)$, or its certificate must strictly enlarge one of $K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B$ by adding the corresponding claim kind, evidence surface, gate pass record, promotion or venue field, discovery-interface row, or command-run tuple. In the latter case, if the enlargement is absent, a finite bad subrecord is returned by Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} or by one of the obstruction types of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}. \end{proposition}

### 1612 corollary cor:publication-safety-current-package-instance [Current-package source-level instance of the safety interface]
\begin{corollary}[Current-package source-level instance of the safety interface] \label{cor:publication-safety-current-package-instance} For the current package, assume the support-surface condition later closed by Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}: the exact supplemental \path{review_bundle/} directory is uploaded with the submission, or an archive-equivalent replacement with matching final digest records is supplied. Under that explicit availability condition, the finite pass-row components of the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} are witnessed by the machine-readable evidence map of Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, the acceptance ledger of Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}, the assigned-gate boundary of Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}, and the four path-complete case witnesses of Proposition~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses}. Therefore the current package instantiates the construction theorem as a certificate-construction and certificate-checking result for the finite records actually supplied, with the bounded package certificate exactness isolated in Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}. \end{corollary}

### 1648 corollary cor:finite-record-gate-soundness-boundary [Finite-record boundary for external gate soundness]
\begin{corollary}[Finite-record boundary for external gate soundness] \label{cor:finite-record-gate-soundness-boundary} Under the hypotheses of the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, the publication-safety conclusion is invariant under replacement of the surrounding implementation by any other implementation with the same submitted acceptance entries, evidence surfaces, promotion fields, discovery-interface rows, and evidence-level boundaries. Therefore the theorem cannot be used, without an additional submitted record-gate-soundness certificate, to infer external workflow correctness, current execution of unrecorded gates, Lean correctness, artifact semantic validity, or venue acceptance. Conversely, any manuscript assertion that tries to promote the finite certificate constructor to one of those stronger implementation claims has a finite overclaim witness: either the required field is absent from \(\mathcal C(c)\), or the row's declared evidence level exceeds the cited certificate surface. \end{corollary}

### 1695 theorem thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion [Exact extension criterion for implementation-soundness readings]
