### 2119 lemma lem:gate-witness-criterion [Gate-witness criterion]
\begin{lemma}[Gate-witness criterion] \label{lem:gate-witness-criterion} A case record is a path-verified witness for the safety interface when it names the source or artifact surface, the invariant that failed, the gate decision, and the safe manuscript lesson. Under the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, such a witness can support a case-study claim, but not a stronger command-run claim, unless the corresponding command log is also recorded. \end{lemma}

### 2138 proposition prop:path-complete-case-witnesses [Path-complete case witnesses]
\begin{proposition}[Path-complete case witnesses] \label{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses} Suppose a case-study row is used as load-bearing evidence for the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}. If the row is path-complete in the sense above, then the row supplies exactly the evidence surface required for a path-verified historical case claim and no evidence surface for a fresh daemon, full-source, theorem-content, artifact-suite, or semantic-substrate rerun. In fact every accepted path-complete case row factors through one of four finite witness skeletons---promotion, route/submission blocking, theorem-content non-growth, or artifact-limitation disclosure---and its admissible manuscript lesson is the unique bounded lesson attached to that skeleton. Replacing that lesson by a fresh experiment, dynamic validation, or semantic artifact claim changes the skeleton by adding a command-run or artifact-semantic coordinate, so the original row no longer certifies the stronger claim. In particular, a promotion row is sufficient only for the bounded claim that an intake packet is not daemon-visible publication work before human promotion; a route-overlap row is sufficient only for the bounded claim that the overlap and submission-state gates blocked advancement until one of the recorded next actions occurs; a theorem-content row is sufficient only for the bounded claim that file churn or compilation did not show substantive theorem growth; and an artifact-limitation row is sufficient only for the bounded claim that the artifact limitation must be disclosed or must block promotion. \end{proposition}

### 2264 proposition prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage [Review-bundle case-snapshot coverage]
\begin{proposition}[Review-bundle case-snapshot coverage] \label{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage} For the four case-study rows in Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}, the review bundle contains a finite external-review surface for every local case path used by the paper. The Newmath block has two local paths, the upper-fibers block has five local paths, the fake-extension block has two local paths, and the Rule110 block has four local paths, for thirteen local-path strings after resolving this paper's relative \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md} to its absolute path in the paper directory. The manifest file \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json} has one \path{case_evidence} entry for each case row and, inside each entry, a \path{reviewable_snapshots} list matching those local paths. File-valued local surfaces are copied into \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/}; directory-valued local surfaces are represented there by directory file-manifests. Consequently the case-study claim made by this paper is externally reviewable at the path-verified snapshot level. It is not a command-run validation claim, a fresh full-source rebuild claim, a daemon-execution claim, or a fresh Rule110 dynamic rerun claim. \end{proposition}

### 2390 proposition audit:fresh-venue-record-criterion [Fresh venue-record criterion]
\begin{proposition}[Fresh venue-record criterion] \label{audit:fresh-venue-record-criterion} Assume that the final submitted support surface contains either the venue-check record \path{VENUE_CHECK.md} and log \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log}, the later local-date rerun log \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-05.log}, or an archive-equivalent replacement naming the same checked URLs, observation time, route rules, command result, source state, environment, exit code, and log path. Then the venue row of Proposition~\ref{audit:venue-submission-pack-gate} is accepted for the current package only when the upload is made on that checked rule state or after a later record with the same fields has replaced it. If that support surface is not submitted, or if an upload occurs after a venue-page change without a replacement record, the package retains only a dated local readiness observation and cannot certify upload-time venue compliance. If the local venue-check files are not part of the submitted support surface, mentions of \path{VENUE_CHECK.md}, \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log}, and \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-05.log} in this supplement are historical/path-verified diagnostic context only, not finite package coordinates for a venue certificate. \end{proposition} \medskip

### 2433 theorem thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation [Venue-rule freshness non-escalation]
\begin{theorem}[Venue-rule freshness non-escalation] \label{thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation} Let $U$ be the finite dated venue record consisting of the official call URL, the submission-form URL, the style-guideline URL, the observation date and time, the HTTP-status run witness \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log} or the later rerun witness \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-05.log}, the observed route class, page limit, proceedings boundary, deadline, supplement access rule, and the local witness rows in Table~\ref{tab:venue-submission-pack-gate}. The venue component of the current finite-record package is accepted exactly for uploads whose venue-rule state is represented by $U$ or by a later replacement record with the same fields. If an upload is made after a venue-page change, or if no upload-time replacement record is supplied, then the package retains only a historical venue observation and cannot certify submission-pack readiness, EasyChair compliance, acceptance, or proceedings status. \end{theorem}

### 2475 corollary cor:historical-venue-record-non-escalation [Historical venue record non-escalation]
\begin{corollary}[Historical venue record non-escalation] \label{cor:historical-venue-record-non-escalation} The venue check recorded at \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log}, with local observation time \path{2026-06-04 10:15 Asia/Singapore}, is a historical venue record. For the present post-2026-06-04 source state it is superseded, for local readiness only, by the rerun log \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-05.log}, whose local observation time is \path{2026-06-05 12:19 Asia/Singapore} and whose exit code is \path{0}. Neither dated record certifies actual upload-time EasyChair compliance, acceptance, or proceedings status unless the final submitted support surface also contains either an upload-time record with the same fields or an explicit statement that the upload used the unchanged venue-rule state represented by the latest dated record. \end{corollary}

### 2504 corollary cor:upload-time-venue-compliance-record-requirement [Upload-time venue-compliance record requirement]
\begin{corollary}[Upload-time venue-compliance record requirement] \label{cor:upload-time-venue-compliance-record-requirement} The logs \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log} and \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-05.log} are dated local-readiness records. For the current package they certify only the venue rule state observed at their recorded observation times. They do not certify upload-time EasyChair compliance unless the final submitted support surface also contains either \begin{enumerate} \item a later upload-time venue/bibliography record with the same URL, observation-time, command-result, source-state, environment, exit-code, and log-path fields; or \item a finite statement that the completed upload used the unchanged venue-rule state represented by the latest included dated record. \end{enumerate} If neither record is present, all venue-dependent conclusions in Proposition~\ref{audit:venue-submission-pack-gate} and Table~\ref{tab:venue-submission-pack-gate} remain historical local-readiness claims and cannot be escalated to upload-time compliance, acceptance, or proceedings-status claims. \end{corollary}

### 2564 proposition audit:venue-submission-pack-gate [Venue-submission pack gate]
\begin{proposition}[Venue-submission pack gate] \label{audit:venue-submission-pack-gate} Assume that, at final upload time, the CICM presentation-only call still has the venue rules listed in Table~\ref{tab:venue-submission-pack-gate}, as certified by the fresh finite record required in Theorem~\ref{thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation}. Then the current package is submission-pack ready for this route exactly when the finite gate in that table passes: the primary artifact is the two-page-plus-bibliography source \path{submission_abstract.tex}, the LNCS-style source and bibliography files are present, the selected exact support surface \path{review_bundle/} is supplied with \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} and the root \path{theorem_inventory.json} coordinate named there, and the title/author metadata remains in the primary source. This gate is a local readiness certificate only; it does not assert EasyChair upload, reviewer approval, acceptance, camera-ready approval, or proceedings publication. \end{proposition} \medskip

### 2626 proposition audit:submission-artifact-role-separation [Submission artifact role separation]
\begin{proposition}[Submission artifact role separation] \label{audit:submission-artifact-role-separation} For the current presentation-only route, the primary artifact, long technical supplement, and review bundle have distinct review roles. The primary source \path{submission_abstract.tex} states the presentation claim, \path{main.tex} proves the long-form interface theorems and records the case tables, and \path{review_bundle/} supplies the supplemental source snapshots, case snapshots, and machine-readable manifest rows. None of these roles converts the support bundle into a second primary submission or converts a primary-artifact claim into an uncited supplement claim. \end{proposition} \medskip

### 2718 proposition prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation [Formal-source gate instantiation]
\begin{proposition}[Formal-source gate instantiation] \label{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation} For the current presentation package, the Lean-facing assertions in rows \path{pc3} and \path{pc4} of Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-inventory} are accepted only as bounded contextual interfaces, not as accepted formal-source claims. The accepted certificate surface is the traceability row $a_1$, the fake-extension boundary row $a_5$, Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}, \path{source_interface_record.json}, \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, and \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json}. These records certify that the short note may mention the mathlib-free design, marker-audit, and axiom-purity audit vocabulary only as source-interface context and only with the evidence boundary stated in rows \path{pc3} and \path{pc4}. They do not certify a fresh Lean build, a current full-source marker audit, an absence-of-axioms theorem, or an accepted axiom-purity pass for the whole newmath tree. Conversely, a Lean-facing sentence in this package can become a current accepted formal-source claim exactly by adding an accepted formal-source certificate row with build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records; if the sentence asserts freshness or currency of an execution, the row must also include the command, source commit or local state, environment, exit code, and log path required for command-run evidence. With the present records, every stronger reading of rows \path{pc3} and \path{pc4} has a minimal bad subrecord of missing formal-source gates or missing command-run fields. \end{proposition}

### 2785 theorem thm:formal-source-promotion-obstruction [Formal-source promotion obstruction]
\begin{theorem}[Formal-source promotion obstruction] \label{thm:formal-source-promotion-obstruction} In the current presentation package, no manuscript sentence about the Lean/BEDC source interface can be promoted from bounded contextual interface evidence to an accepted formal-source claim unless the certificate is enlarged by an accepted formal-source row containing build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records. If a fresh execution reading is also asserted, the certificate must additionally contain the command, source commit or final package digest manifest, environment, exit code, and log path required for command-run evidence. With the present six-row package ledger, every such promotion attempt has a finite bad subrecord and is rejected by the schema verifier. \end{theorem}

### 2826 theorem thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction [Evidence-level overclaim obstruction]
\begin{theorem}[Evidence-level overclaim obstruction] \label{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction} Fix the certificate schema, dispatch function, and evidence-level vocabulary of this paper. Let $c$ be a load-bearing claim whose accepted certificate has level path-verified and whose boundary field $B$ records that no command-run assertion is made. Then every stronger reading of $c$ that asserts any of the following additional conclusions is rejected by the finite schema verifier: \begin{enumerate} \item external gate soundness for an arbitrary implementation of the workflow; \item Lean correctness, full-source rebuild success, or axiom-purity beyond the recorded formal-source gate fields; \item end-to-end external semantic validity of artifact data beyond the finite manifest and evaluator surfaces named in $E$ and $R$; \item a fresh daemon run, full-source rerun, Rule110 or GroundCompiler rerun, or journal or workshop acceptance; or \item bundle-backed reviewability when neither \path{review_bundle/} nor an archival equivalent carrying the same finite records is in the submitted artifact boundary. \end{enumerate} More precisely, such a stronger reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord of one of the five types in Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}. Hence the accepted claim is rigid at its declared evidence level: path-verified certificate records cannot be promoted by prose into command-run evidence, semantic validation, formal-source correctness, or external acceptance. \end{theorem}

### 2901 theorem thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface [Finite-witness manifest-bounded record interface]
\begin{theorem}[Finite-witness manifest-bounded record interface] \label{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface} This theorem is a manifest-bounded finite-record interface instance of Theorem~\ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}: it records only the bounded manifest/evaluator coordinates that may be checked, and it rejects stronger artifact readings unless the package supplies the additional finite records required for them, such as semantic-validation pass records or fresh rerun logs. The interface domain is the finite set \[ \mathcal M_{a_6}=\{\text{manifest entry},\text{local paths}, \text{reviewable snapshots},\text{boundary field}, \text{status pass row},\text{limitation-ledger pass row}\}. \] No Rule110 or GroundCompiler property outside $\mathcal M_{a_6}$ is asserted. For the current presentation package, assuming the named review-bundle records are supplied as supplemental material or by an accepted archive-equivalent surface, the finite-witness assertions in rows \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and \path{pc11} of Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-inventory} have the following bounded interface. The assertions printed in this source are manuscript-source assertions about the allowed finite-record reading; the path checks against the status files, limitation ledgers, and machine-readable manifest are supplemental-record assertions conditional on the named \path{review_bundle/} records being submitted and matching those paths. Row \path{pc5} is contextual: it states the evidence discipline that finite witnesses are bound by manifest rows, evaluator outcomes, and substrate contracts rather than by Lean alone. Rows \path{pc6} and \path{pc11} are accepted only as the bounded artifact lesson $a_6$. Their named manifest and evaluator surfaces are \path{manifest:case...

