### 3394 theorem thm:exact-inclusion-support-surface-realization [Exact-inclusion support-surface realization]
\begin{theorem}[Exact-inclusion support-surface realization] \label{thm:exact-inclusion-support-surface-realization} For the current package the selected support branch is exact supplemental inclusion of \path{review_bundle/}, but this branch remains conditional until an external upload-or-archive instantiation record exists. If the completed submission contains that directory with the certificate-file digests listed in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, then every bundle-backed claim in this supplement has a submitted finite support surface at its stated path-verified evidence level. If the exact directory is not submitted, then the same support conclusion holds only after replacing it by an archive satisfying the five tests of Table~\ref{tab:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}; no venue upload receipt, public DOI, or archive locator is claimed for the present package. \end{theorem}

### 3431 theorem thm:final-support-surface-closure [Final support-surface closure]
\begin{theorem}[Final support-surface closure] \label{thm:final-support-surface-closure} For the current package, using Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention}, Proposition~\ref{prop:exact-review-bundle-upload-witness}, and Theorem~\ref{thm:exact-inclusion-support-surface-realization}, the bundle-backed claims in this supplement are admissible at the stated path-verified evidence level exactly when the submitted supplemental material contains the exact directory \path{review_bundle/} whose final packaging record is \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}. A replacement archive is not a current package branch; it would have to satisfy Table~\ref{tab:review-bundle-archive-equivalence} and provide a locator before a future package could select it. If the exact directory is not present in the current package submission, the bundle-backed claims are not part of the accepted support surface, although the short presentation artifact may still cite the pinned public \path{newmath} repository for the imported source spine. \end{theorem}

### 3490 proposition prop:final-immutable-package-state-certificate [Final immutable package-state certificate]
\begin{proposition}[Final immutable package-state certificate] \label{prop:final-immutable-package-state-certificate} For the current presentation package, the final source-state record for the three fresh verifier runs is the pair consisting of the automath repository commit \path{305ee93353b16babc461bfad1a7aee9c808ee268} and the digest manifest \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}; the fresh venue/bibliography live-check run carries the same digest manifest as its package-state boundary and records the absolute local query date 2026-06-05. The selected supplemental support mode is conditional exact inclusion of \path{review_bundle/}; no DOI-bearing, institutional, or other archival replacement is claimed. Consequently a command-result claim for any of the four logs \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}, \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log}, or \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-05.log}, and \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-05.log} is accepted only when read against the declared package-state boundary and the exact included bundle once the external instantiation record exists. If either the digest manifest is absent or the exact bundle is not submitted and no archive satisfies Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}, the corresponding command-result and bundle-backed support claims are outside the accepted support surface of this package. \end{proposition}

### 3543 theorem thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation [Workspace-to-final-package reconciliation]
\begin{theorem}[Workspace-to-final-package reconciliation] \label{thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation} Let $H_0$ be the automath commit \path{305ee93353b16babc461bfad1a7aee9c808ee268}, and let $D_0$ be the digest manifest \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}. The source-state claim made in Proposition~\ref{prop:final-immutable-package-state-certificate} is the finite package claim $(H_0,D_0)$, not a claim that every later reviewer checkout, editor workspace, or post-packaging working tree has Git \path{HEAD} equal to $H_0$. If a current workspace reports a different Git \path{HEAD}, then the verifier-log and bundle-backed claims of this supplement remain admissible exactly in the following two reconciled cases: \begin{enumerate} \item the submitted support surface is exact inclusion of \path{review_bundle/}, or an archive equivalent to it, whose finite certificate files match the SHA-256 rows of $D_0$; or \item the package is deliberately advanced to the current workspace state by rerunning the three verifier commands and replacing the displayed source-state record, the verifier logs, and the digest manifest by a new common commit--manifest certificate. \end{enumerate} In all other cases a mismatch between a later workspace \path{HEAD} and $H_0$ does not certify a new package state and does not invalidate the historical finite package claim $(H_0,D_0)$; it only shows that the later workspace is outside the submitted support surface. \end{theorem}

### 3601 proposition prop:mutable-checkout-observation-boundary [Mutable-checkout observations are non-certifying]
\begin{proposition}[Mutable-checkout observations are non-certifying] \label{prop:mutable-checkout-observation-boundary} Let $W$ be a review-time working tree whose Git \path{HEAD} is not the commit $H_0$ named in Theorem~\ref{thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation}. The observation of $W$ is not a certificate coordinate for any verifier-log claim in this package unless $W$ is promoted by the second branch of that theorem, namely by rerunning the three verifier commands and replacing the commit, log, and digest records by a new common finite package certificate. Consequently, a reviewer who sees $W$ with a different \path{HEAD} has exactly one source-level reconciliation test for the existing package: inspect the exact-bundle or archive-equivalent surface whose certificate-file bytes match \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}. \end{proposition}

### 3630 theorem thm:support-surface-normal-form [Support-surface normal form for bundle-backed claims]
\begin{theorem}[Support-surface normal form for bundle-backed claims] \label{thm:support-surface-normal-form} For the current presentation package, every accepted use of a bundle-backed certificate coordinate is in exactly one of the following normal forms. \begin{enumerate} \item \emph{Exact inclusion}: the submitted support surface contains the exact \path{review_bundle/} directory whose certificate-file bytes match \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}. \item \emph{Archive-equivalent inclusion}: a replacement archive satisfies every row of Table~\ref{tab:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}, including manifest identity, bundle-version identity, finite file-list identity, SHA-256 content identity, and relative path semantics. \item \emph{Promoted rerun}: the package is advanced to a new source state by rerunning the verifier commands and replacing the displayed source-state record, verifier logs, and digest manifest by one common finite commit--manifest certificate. \end{enumerate} If none of these three normal forms is present, the attempted bundle-backed reading has a finite obstruction subrecord: either the exact directory is absent, the archive-equivalence table has a failed row, or the rerun branch lacks the command, source-state, environment, exit-code, log-path, and digest coordinates required by the command-boundary rule. Thus the late support-surface chain is a finite audit-interface classification for this package, not a theorem about general archive infrastructure or about semantic correctness of the bundled artifacts. \end{theorem}

### 3694 theorem thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary [Upload instantiation boundary]
\begin{theorem}[Upload instantiation boundary] \label{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary} Before the bundle-backed claims of this supplement are treated as part of a completed venue submission, the external submission record must instantiate the support-surface branch selected by Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention} and Theorems~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} and \ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form}: it must record that \path{review_bundle/} was uploaded exactly with digest manifest $D_0$ and with the root \path{theorem_inventory.json} coordinate named by the manifest and ledger. The TeX source and the local digest manifest select that exact-inclusion branch but do not constitute a venue upload receipt. In the present source package no such external receipt is recorded; consequently all bundle-backed claims are proved only as conditional source-level support claims until the missing external exact-inclusion record is supplied. \end{theorem}

### 3730 corollary cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record [Upload-or-archive instantiation record]
\begin{corollary}[Upload-or-archive instantiation record] \label{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} The bundle-backed support claims of the current package may be used only under the external-record condition of Theorem~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary}. In particular, \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} is a local byte-level packaging manifest and not, by itself, an upload receipt or an archive locator; no archive replacement branch is selected for this source package. \end{corollary}

### 3745 corollary cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation [Current no-receipt support-surface non-escalation]
\begin{corollary}[Current no-receipt support-surface non-escalation] \label{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation} In the current source package the records \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json}, and \path{theorem_inventory.json} do not contain a venue upload receipt, public DOI, institutional archive identifier, or archive-equivalent locator for \path{review_bundle/}. Hence every claim whose evidence surface is a bundle-local path is accepted only as a conditional exact-supplemental-inclusion claim. Such a claim becomes an externally instantiated submitted-support claim only after one of the two finite records below is added to the submitted support surface: \begin{enumerate} \item an upload receipt naming the exact \path{review_bundle/} directory and the final digest manifest; or \item an archive-equivalent locator satisfying the file-list, digest, manifest, and path-semantics tests of Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}. \end{enumerate} Until then, the theorem inventory, verifier logs, case snapshots, source snapshot, and primary-claim inventory remain local/path-verified review-bundle coordinates rather than unconditional public or venue-submitted coordinates. \end{corollary}

### 3813 proposition prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary [Manual theorem-inventory boundary]
\begin{proposition}[Manual theorem-inventory boundary] \label{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} The file \path{theorem_inventory.json} is a manual Stage-A inventory record rather than a deterministic theorem-environment extractor output. Its accepted role in the current theorem chain is finite obligation traceability: it enumerates the theorem-like obligations selected by the Stage-A review and connects that finite list to the manifest and acceptance ledger. It is not an executable completeness certificate for all \LaTeX{} theorem environments in \path{main.tex}; no extractor command, extractor log, or theorem-environment coverage claim is part of the current submitted support surface. \end{proposition}

### 3850 proposition prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure [Regenerated theorem-inventory digest closure]
\begin{proposition}[Regenerated theorem-inventory digest closure] \label{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure} Suppose a Stage-A pass regenerates either \path{theorem_inventory.json} or \path{theorem_inventory.md} after the package has selected the exact-inclusion support branch. Then the final support-surface claims of Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}, the submitted theorem-ledger claim of Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}, and the command boundary of Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory} remain closed only after the package refreshes the corresponding SHA-256 rows in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} and reruns the finite record verifiers whose records name that digest manifest or inventory coordinate. Once those rows and verifier logs are refreshed, the regeneration creates no new mathematical or executable extractor claim: it only changes the byte-coordinate of the manual theorem-obligation ledger inside the already selected finite support surface. \end{proposition}

### 3896 theorem thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary [Seven-row submitted theorem-obligation ledger integration]
\begin{theorem}[Seven-row submitted theorem-obligation ledger integration] \label{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} Let $P^{+}_{7}$ be the seven-row support state obtained from $P^{+}_{6}$ by adding exactly the manual submitted theorem-ledger coordinate \path{a7}. In $P^{+}_{7}$, the theorem-obligation ledger is no longer a detached review-only artifact: the submitted finite support surface contains \path{theorem_inventory.json} through the manifest field \path{theorem_inventory} and through acceptance-ledger entry \path{a7}. The earlier six-row state $P^{+}_{6}$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} remains the row-frozen certificate state for \path{a1}--\path{a6}; the present theorem adds exactly the manual theorem-ledger coordinate \path{a7}. Consequently the package may make the following bounded theorem-ledger claim: every theorem-obligation assertion made by the submission is supported only through the finite coordinates in Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger}, namely the integrated theorem inventory, the primary-claim inventory, the certificate ledger, and the manifest exposure records. By Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary}, this is a manual Stage-A obligation ledger claim, not a deterministic theorem-environment extraction or completeness claim. The claim does not assert that \path{theorem_inventory.json} proves the mathematical semantics of each theorem, does not replace the proofs printed in this source, and does not certify a Lean rebuild, publication-daemon run, Rule110 artifact rerun, extractor run, or external upload. \end{theorem}

### 3960 proposition prop:submitted-theorem-inventory-availability [Submitted theorem-inventory availability]
\begin{proposition}[Submitted theorem-inventory availability] \label{prop:submitted-theorem-inventory-availability} If the external support-surface condition of Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} is satisfied, then \path{theorem_inventory.json} is an available submitted certificate coordinate for the bounded manual theorem-obligation ledger claim of Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}. If the exact bundle is not uploaded with matching final digests, the theorem inventory has only local diagnostic force and cannot support a submitted theorem-ledger claim in the current package. \end{proposition}

### 3986 proposition audit:review-bundle-availability-condition [Review-bundle availability condition]
\begin{proposition}[Review-bundle availability condition] \label{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition} The final support mode selected for the current presentation package is exact supplemental inclusion of \path{review_bundle/}, conditional on the external instantiation record of Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}, as recorded in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} and in Proposition~\ref{prop:final-immutable-package-state-certificate}. This is a conditional support-mode selection, not a completed venue-upload record or public archive locator. The source-interface, case-snapshot, primary-claim inventory, submission-interface map, theorem-inventory, and verifier-record claims are therefore reviewable at the path-verified evidence level stated in this supplement only under the exact included bundle once the external instantiation record exists. A future archival equivalent would have to satisfy Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}, but it is not the current package branch. If the bundle is not uploaded exactly with matching final digests, then the primary note retains only the public-source citation to the pinned newmath repository and the architectural claim in the short text; the supplemental support claims depending on bundle-local files are not accepted by this package. \end{proposition} \medskip

### 4038 proposition audit:case-snapshot-non-rerun-boundary [Case-snapshot non-rerun boundary]
\begin{proposition}[Case-snapshot non-rerun boundary] \label{audit:case-snapshot-non-rerun-boundary} Under the hypotheses of Propositions~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses} and \ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}, the four case-study rows cannot be read as fresh dynamic-behavior claims. Any stronger reading asserting a fresh publication-daemon execution, full-source rebuild, theorem-content rerun, Rule110 dynamic rerun, or GroundCompiler semantic rerun requires command, source state, environment, exit-code, and log-path fields in addition to the bundled snapshots. Without those fields the stronger reading is rejected by the finite record verifier at its evidence-strength boundary. \end{proposition} \medskip

### 4106 proposition audit:command-run-boundary-inventory [Command-run boundary inventory]
\begin{proposition}[Command-run boundary inventory] \label{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory} For the current presentation package, Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory} is complete for the command-boundary claims used by Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, and the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}. The accepted fresh command-run claims in the package are exactly the three finite record-verifier invocations recorded in \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} and \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log}, and \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-05.log}, together with the live venue and bibliography freshness check recorded in \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-05.log}. The Lean/BEDC source theorem semantics, publication-daemon or route-state surfaces, and Rule110/\allowbreak GroundCompiler artifact surfaces remain path-verified, historical, or bounded-artifact evidence and are not command-run validation claims. \end{proposition} \medskip
